def create_logger(app):
    logger = logging.getLogger("flask.app")

    if app.debug and logger.level == logging.NOTSET:
        logger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)

    if not has_level_handler(logger):
        logger.addHandler(default_handler)

    return logger
```

And the `logger` property in `app.py`:

```python
@locked_cached_property
def logger(self):
    return create_logger(self)
```

The issue is that `create_logger` always gets (or creates) a single logger named `"flask.app"`. Since it's always the same logger name, all Flask apps share the same logger instance. When you call `app1.logger.addHandler(handler1)` and `app2.logger.addHandler(handler2)`, both handlers are added to the same `"flask.app"` logger, so both apps' log messages get sent to both handlers.

The fix would be to make the logger name unique per application instance. Instead of using `"flask.app"` as the logger name, it should use something like `"flask.app.{app.name}"` or `"flask.app.{id(app)}"` to differentiate between different Flask instances.

